riched20: draw auto-colored text readably on a background the app chose (Acrobat comment text)

Reported: in Adobe Acrobat Pro 21.001.20135, comment text (sticky-note pop-ups on the page and
comment cards in the Comment pane, including a reply while it is typed and after it is posted) is drawn
so faint it can barely be read.

Mechanism (SOLID, +richedit + relay trace, lab log acrobat-at-1-20261107-153455.log): those are
windowed RICHEDIT50W controls. Acrobat sets their background itself (EM_SETBKGNDCOLOR 0xf5f5f5 for
the pop-up and posted card, 0xffffff for the card being edited), fills them through TOM
(ITextSelection::SetText) and leaves the text CFE_AUTOCOLOR; it paints them via WM_PRINTCLIENT into
its own buffer. riched20's draw_text resolves the auto color through the host's
TxGetSysColor(COLOR_WINDOWTEXT) = GetSysColor(8) = 0xe0e0e0 under Neutron's dark palette and calls
SetTextColor(0xe0e0e0): 224 gray on 245, contrast 1.21:1 (1.32:1 on white).

Windows: CHARFORMAT's documentation says CFE_AUTOCOLOR text "is the return value of
GetSysColor(COLOR_WINDOWTEXT)", so Windows with the same palette would draw the same faint text;
Windows' default palette has COLOR_WINDOWTEXT black, which is what Acrobat is designed against. The
dark palette is deliberate and shared by every Wine-drawn dialog and edit control, so the palette
stays; the unreadable text is fixed instead (as user32's DrawTextExW does for Button/Static text).

Fix: get_text_color() passes auto-colored text through readable_auto_color(): when it would have
less than 2:1 contrast against the background this control paints under it (the run's own
background color, else the host's COLOR_WINDOW, i.e. the EM_SETBKGNDCOLOR color), it is drawn black
on a light background (Windows' default COLOR_WINDOWTEXT) or white on a dark one. Text that already
reads is never touched; with Windows' default palette auto text on a light background always reads,
so this acts only where the result was unreadable. Selected text, links and explicit text colors
are unchanged; TOM's ITextFont::GetForeColor still reports COLOR_WINDOWTEXT.

Not covered: a transparent windowless host (TXTBACK_TRANSPARENT) paints its own background, which
riched20 cannot see, so auto text there is left alone. Text drawn by apps themselves (not rich edit)
is out of scope.

Lab evidence (runtime 11.10-185at = 185ao + this, desk at 168 dpi, fresh reflinks of AO-1, test PDF
with one sticky note; kill-switch A/B on the same build, n=2 each arm, pixel contrast from
captures, WCAG ratio):
  pop-up note text      fix 14.0:1, 14.0:1   switch off 1.17:1, 1.17:1
  comment card text     fix 20.3:1, 20.3:1   switch off 1.28:1, 1.28:1
  reply being typed     fix 21:1 x2          switch off 224 on 255 (caret only dark)
  posted reply          fix 21:1 x2          switch off 1.32:1 x2
File menu and Tools page: 0 pixels differ between arms (n=2); Home page: only the "opened" time
differs (n=1); document view: only the pop-up text line differs. Lightroom Classic (also uses rich
edit; EM_SETBKGNDCOLOR 0 = black): identity plate and Metadata fields identical between arms (n=1).

Kill switch: NEUTRON_RICHED_AUTOCOLOR=0.
--- a/dlls/riched20/paint.c
+++ b/dlls/riched20/paint.c
@@ -203,6 +203,60 @@
     return offs;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: auto-colored rich edit text on a background the app chose (Acrobat's comments).
+ *
+ * Text with CFE_AUTOCOLOR is drawn in GetSysColor(COLOR_WINDOWTEXT), as on Windows. Acrobat's
+ * sticky-note pop-ups and comment cards are rich edit controls with auto-colored text on a light
+ * background the app sets itself (EM_SETBKGNDCOLOR 0xf5f5f5 / 0xffffff). On Windows' default
+ * palette COLOR_WINDOWTEXT is black, so the text reads; Neutron's dark palette makes it 224 gray,
+ * and the comment text came out 224 on 245, about 1.2:1. The palette has to stay dark (every
+ * Wine-drawn edit control and dialog uses it), so fix the text, not the palette: when auto-colored
+ * text would have almost no contrast against the background this control itself paints under it,
+ * draw it black on a light background (Windows' default COLOR_WINDOWTEXT) or white on a dark one.
+ * Text that already reads is never touched, and on Windows' default palette auto text on a light
+ * background always reads, so this only acts where the result was unreadable. A transparent
+ * control's background is painted by its host and is unknown here, so it is left alone.
+ * NEUTRON_RICHED_AUTOCOLOR=0 turns this off. */
+static BOOL readable_auto_color_enabled(void)
+{
+    static LONG enabled = -1;
+
+    if (enabled < 0)
+    {
+        char env[8];
+        DWORD len = GetEnvironmentVariableA( "NEUTRON_RICHED_AUTOCOLOR", env, sizeof(env) );
+        enabled = !(len && len < sizeof(env) && env[0] == '0');
+    }
+    return enabled;
+}
+
+static int luma1000( COLORREF c )
+{
+    /* sRGB relative luminance x1000, gamma approximated as 2 (as user32's DrawTextExW check) */
+    int r = GetRValue(c), g = GetGValue(c), b = GetBValue(c);
+    return (2126 * r * r + 7152 * g * g + 722 * b * b) / (255 * 255 * 10);
+}
+
+static COLORREF readable_auto_color( ME_Context *c, ME_Style *style, COLORREF text )
+{
+    COLORREF back;
+    int lt, lb, hi, lo;
+
+    if (!readable_auto_color_enabled()) return text;
+    if ((style->fmt.dwMask & CFM_BACKCOLOR) && !(style->fmt.dwEffects & CFE_AUTOBACKCOLOR))
+        back = style->fmt.crBackColor;
+    else if (editor_opaque( c->editor ))
+        back = ITextHost_TxGetSysColor( c->editor->texthost, COLOR_WINDOW );
+    else
+        return text;
+
+    lt = luma1000( text ); lb = luma1000( back );
+    hi = max( lt, lb ); lo = min( lt, lb );
+    if ((hi + 50) * 10 >= (lo + 50) * 20) return text;   /* contrast ratio 2:1 or better */
+    /* black or white, whichever contrasts more with the background */
+    return (lb + 50) * (lb + 50) >= 50 * 1050 ? RGB(0,0,0) : RGB(255,255,255);
+}
+
 static COLORREF get_text_color( ME_Context *c, ME_Style *style, BOOL highlight )
 {
     COLORREF color;
@@ -212,7 +266,8 @@
     else if ((style->fmt.dwMask & CFM_LINK) && (style->fmt.dwEffects & CFE_LINK))
         color = RGB(0,0,255);
     else if ((style->fmt.dwMask & CFM_COLOR) && (style->fmt.dwEffects & CFE_AUTOCOLOR))
-        color = ITextHost_TxGetSysColor( c->editor->texthost, COLOR_WINDOWTEXT );
+        color = readable_auto_color( c, style,
+                                     ITextHost_TxGetSysColor( c->editor->texthost, COLOR_WINDOWTEXT ) );
     else
         color = style->fmt.crTextColor;
 
